  <section class="wrap fiche-body">
    <div>
      <h2>The vibe</h2>
      <p>Clinique Vétérinaire du Careï, at 28 Val du Careï in Menton, was founded in 1994 by Drs Sandrine Franzolini and Werner Glavinaz. With Dr Fabrice Loumeau (a partner since 2007) and Dr Zéphyr Bernard, it forms a single team with the Carnoles clinic in Roquebrune-Cap-Martin (167 avenue Pasteur, 04 92 10 32 11), which shares the same website.</p>

      <h2>What to know before you go</h2>
      <p>According to the official website of the Careï and Carnoles clinics, the Careï clinic receives patients Monday to Friday from 8am to 12pm then from 2:30pm to 7pm, and on Saturday from 8:30am to 2pm (Carnoles: Saturday from 8:30am to 12pm). Phone: 04 92 10 03 00.</p>
      <p>The website presents four vets and four assistants, and details the services: annual health visit with vaccine updates, ultrasound (abdomen, heart, pregnancy monitoring), surgical unit, dentistry (ultrasonic scaling), blood tests carried out on site with results in fifteen to twenty minutes, food and hygiene products on free access during opening hours, and home visits for people who cannot travel. According to Mappy, the clientele includes dogs, cats and small pets, and the clinic has reserved parking.</p>
      <p>Emergencies: the website's hours grid says "Nights, Sundays and public holidays: emergencies", and specifies that outside opening hours you are put through to the emergency service by calling 04 92 10 03 00; the website does not say who provides this service or until what time it answers.</p>
      <div class="callout">Coco's tip: in Menton, at the far end of the department, it's best to have a reliable vet address in mind before heading off into the hinterland with your dog.</div>

      <h2>Nearby</h2>
      <p>Menton town centre and its seafront are just a few minutes from the clinic.</p>
